{
 "id": "2024-malewczyk-facade",
 "name": "Facade Composition Regularity Rating",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original images: twelve abstract graphics of black rectangles on a white background, made by the authors to stand for the six types of facade composition (A-F) of contemporary Polish multifamily residential buildings, two examples per type (A1, A2 ... F1, F2); type A is a fully even grid and type F has elements of varied sizes with random spacing. As in the paper's online questionnaire, participants go through four sets of six graphics, each set showing one example of each composition type at the same time in the order of the paper's Table 1, and rate each graphic on a 6-point scale from 1 (the most irregular) to 6 (the most regular), so that each graphic is rated twice. Gender, age and education are asked at the end. Rebuilt from Malewczyk, Taraszkiewicz & Czyż (2024), Visual Perception of Regularity and the Composition Pattern Type of the Facade, Buildings 14(5): 1389, with the graphics cropped from its Figures 2-7 and the set order and scale from its Section 2.2 and Table 1 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: A1-F2; answers are in regularity, keyed by set and graphic (e.g. s1_A1 for set 1, graphic A1). Analysis as in the paper: one-way ANOVA of the regularity rating by composition type and by graphic with Dunnett's T3 post hoc tests, mean ratings per type, and a repeated-measures GLM for the two ratings of each graphic; the paper (48 first-year architecture and spatial management students in Gdańsk, Cronbach's alpha .93 over the 24 ratings) found type A rated most regular (M = 5.84), then C (5.16), B (4.05) and D (3.70), which did not differ significantly, E (2.23) and F least regular (1.40). Differences from the original study: (1) the paper ran on QuestionPro for students of one faculty; here anyone can take part; (2) the question wording, the instruction text and the gender, age and education questions are ours (the paper says only that these three were asked); (3) the set order follows Table 1 as printed, in which B2 appears three times and B1 once although the text says each graphic was rated twice; (4) the graphics are the figure copies, cropped to the rectangles and re-centred on a white square, not the 1200 x 1200 px originals.",
